// THE SAME KEY ERROR AT THE RUNTIME WALK. The compile-time walk stopped
// asking "does this handler cover the effect" at pin 5c39a1a7; the runtime
// one still does — `ev_perform_node` keys on `key@0`, the interned EFFECT,
// and the chain carries one node per effect GROUP.
//
// This shape reaches it. `call_b` puts the perform behind a frame fence, so
// the lexical walk cannot answer; a second declarer of `b` makes the op
// ambiguous, so there is no unique handler name for the singleton walk
// either; and the installed pair covers ONE effect's DISJOINT op sets with
// the non-declaring handler innermost. The effect-keyed walk therefore stops
// at `ha`, whose group for `Two` holds one arm — and `b`'s slot is 1, past
// the end of it.
//
// Where the compile-time version of this emitted a call to a named arm that
// does not exist and was caught by the assembler, this one indexes past a
// live arm region and dispatches on whatever is there. Expected: 11 + 22 = 33.
effect Two {
  a(n: Int) -> Int
  b(n: Int) -> Int
}

handler ha {
  a(n) => resume(n + 1),
}

handler hb {
  b(n) => resume(n + 2),
}

// A second declarer of `b`, never installed — its only job is to make the op
// ambiguous so the singleton tier declines and the evidence tier answers.
handler hb_alt {
  b(n) => resume(n + 3),
}

fn call_b(n) = b(n)

fn main() = a(10) + call_b(20) ~> ha ~> hb
